-- 1900_ma_wrong_bill_cohort_enacted_vehicle_season2.sql
-- The Massachusetts wrong-bill cohort. 150 keys written: 80 citation repairs + 70 blanks.
-- 71 further keys in the same cohort are BLOCKED and are listed at the foot of this header.
--
-- 🔴 WHY A FORWARD WRITE. Season 1 is CLOSED and IMMUTABLE. A blank is `value 0` INSERTED into
-- the OPEN season, never a DELETE -- a deleted Season 2 row falls back to Season 1 and the bad
-- chair stays visible (CC_0057). Every row written CARRIES THE SOURCES IT EXAMINED (mig 1887),
-- and every repaired row has its PROSE rewritten too, not just its URLs: the defective prose
-- names the false bill out loud ("voted for the ROE Act (H.4998, 2020)"), so swapping only the
-- citation would leave a row whose words still point at a retirement-credit bill for one named
-- individual. That is the stale-prose-after-a-fix defect from migration 1730.
--
-- ══ WHAT THIS COHORT IS ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- A sweep of all 676 bills this corpus cites found 262 citations, across 17 bills and 260 stance
-- rows, pointing at a REAL BUT UNRELATED bill. Migration 1899 wrote the 39 H.4264 rows. This
-- migration writes the rest: 221 rows, of which 150 are writable and 71 are blocked.
-- 🔑 THE CORRECT NUMBER IS DEMONSTRABLY KNOWN TO THE CORPUS -- for most of these acts it cites
-- BOTH a right and a wrong number -- which is what makes these REPAIRABLE rather than simply
-- wrong. The claim is usually TRUE and only the citation is false. 80 of 150 were repaired.
--
-- ══ THE FINDING THAT SHAPED EVERY VERDICT ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 🔴🔴 FOR A VOTE CLAIM, THE CORRECT INSTRUMENT IS ALMOST NEVER THE BILL THAT CARRIES THE ACT'S
-- NAME. A Massachusetts landmark act is filed under one number, amended, REPRINTED under another,
-- sent across, and finally enacted as a conference report under a number nobody recognises --
-- and the bill the public knows may never be voted on at all:
--   * THE ROE ACT'S OWN BILL DIED. H.3320 of the 191st: "No further action taken", 1/5/2021. It
--     never reached a floor vote. The ROE Act became law as H.5179, "An Act providing for access
--     to reproductive health services", section 40 of the FY21 budget, over the Governor's veto
--     -- Chapter 263 of the Acts of 2020. House 107-46 on the override, Senate 32-8.
--   * POLICE REFORM: S.2800 -> S.2820 -> S.2963, Chapter 253 of 2020. House 91-67 on enactment,
--     107-51 on re-enactment, Senate 28-12 on the conference report.
--   * VOTES ACT: S.2545 -> S.2554 -> S.2924, Chapter 92 of 2022. House 126-29, Senate 37-3.
--   * AFFORDABLE HOMES: H.4977, Chapter 150 of 2024 -- the one act here whose familiar number IS
--     the enacted vehicle. House 128-24, Senate 37-2.
-- A sponsorship claim is tested against the FILED bill's cosponsor list; a vote claim against the
-- ENACTED vehicle's roll call. They are different documents answering different questions, and a
-- first pass that checked sponsorship first "confirmed" eight rows saying "voted for the ROE Act"
-- by finding the member on H.3320's cosponsor list. Those are not the same fact.
--
-- ══ THE BLOCKER MIGRATION 1899 LEFT, AND THE FIX ═════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 1899 recorded that MA roll-call PDFs "can prove a NAY, never a YEA": `pdftotext -layout`
-- recovered 14/14 nays but only 39 of 144 yeas on S.9, so most of this cohort could only have
-- been blanked -- over-retiring claims that are probably TRUE.
-- 🔑 THE CAUSE WAS NOT THE PDF. The roll call is a four-column grid whose THIRD COLUMN sits at a
-- slightly different vertical offset, so -layout assigns every second name of that column to the
-- gap line BETWEEN grid rows, where it is orphaned from its Y/N marker. Xpdf's pdftotext has a
-- `-table` mode that groups words into grid CELLS by position instead of by raster line; every
-- name then lands beside its own marker. Tokenise the cells on runs of 2+ spaces rather than
-- regexing the line (a boundary regex drops "--Jones--", which does not start with a letter, and
-- swallows the next cell after "Farley-Bouvier", which is two spaces from the next marker).
-- ALL TEN ROLL CALLS USED HERE PARSE EXACTLY, verified twice: against the PDF's own declared
-- totals AND against the totals the bill history states independently.
-- 🔴 A TOTALS CHECK IS NOT ENOUGH ON ITS OWN. Senate roll calls are a different document -- a
-- two-column list of full names under YEAS./NAYS. headings. A name-shaped regex over the
-- flattened text split "Creem, Cynthia Stone" and "Lovely, Joan B." into "Creem, Cynthia" and
-- "Stone Lovely, Joan B.". That error LOSES NO NAME AND GAINS NONE, so 40 parsed still equalled
-- 40 declared while two senators carried the wrong surname. A count that matches does not mean
-- the boundaries are right.
--
-- ══ THE EIGHT VERDICTS ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
--   41 VOTE_CONFIRMED      REPAIR. The member voted as the row says, on the enacted vehicle, and
--                          the vote was DIVIDED. Chair unchanged, citation and prose rewritten.
--   28 COSPONSOR_CONFIRMED REPAIR. The member is on the correct bill's Cosponsors page.
--   11 DISSENT_CONFIRMED   REPAIR. The member voted NAY and the row says so. 🔑 THE CONVERSE OF
--                          THE LEHMAN RULE: dissent from a lopsided majority IS distinctive.
--   24 NOT_DISTINCTIVE     BLANK. The vote is verified but near-unanimous. 🔑 A Yea on a 95-5
--                          vote distinguishes a member from almost nobody -- the rule that
--                          blanked Newhouse on 385-41 (1894) and Dingell on 417-10 (1896).
--   20 NOT_SERVING         BLANK. The member was not in the legislature. IMPOSSIBLE, not merely
--                          unsupported.
--   14 LADDER_SCOPE        BLANK, finding preserved. See below -- the voting-rights ladder.
--   10 NOT_A_COSPONSOR     BLANK. A CHECKED absence, against every filing of the act since the
--                          190th General Court, not only the court the row names.
--    1 INVERTED_VOTE       BLANK. Russell E. Holmes's row says he "voted for the ROE Act" and
--                          seats chair 1. 🔴🔴 HE VOTED NAY, on both the re-enactment (#317) and
--                          the veto override (#374). There is exactly one Holmes on the roll
--                          call, so no disambiguation was needed. Trusting the citation would
--                          have left an inverted chair standing; retiring on the false citation
--                          alone would have been right for the wrong reason.
--    1 CHAIR_NOT_SUPPORTED BLANK. Hannah E. Kane / public-safety-approach. Her row does not claim
--                          she voted for the act -- it says she "took a moderate position" -- so
--                          calling it an inverted vote would put words in the row's mouth. She
--                          voted NAY twice. That is consistent with the DIRECTION of her prose
--                          and establishes nothing for the MIDDLE chair the row seats.
--
-- ══ 🔴🔴 THE LADDER-SCOPE BLANKS: SEASON 2 REPLACED THE VOTING-RIGHTS LADDER ═══════════════════
-- A REPAIR MUST NOT MOVE A CHAIR, AND IN SEASON 2 THAT IS NOT AUTOMATIC. Where the row already
-- has a Season 2 row the context is updated in place at ITS value. Where it does not, carrying
-- the Season 1 value into Season 2 only means the same thing if THE RUNG'S OWN TEXT at that value
-- still says the same thing. Every carried chair was checked that way; 58 rungs are
-- character-for-character identical and 4 were reworded without changing what they ask (named
-- explicitly in guard 4 below, so the exemption is auditable).
-- VOTING-RIGHTS WAS NOT REWORDED, IT WAS REPLACED. Season 1 ran "automatically register all
-- eligible citizens to vote and allow online voting" / "expand early voting periods and make
-- mail-in voting available to all" -- the subject of the VOTES Act. Season 2 is a pure VOTER-ID
-- scale: rung 1 is "Require no identification to vote, verifying voters by signature", rung 4 is
-- "Require photo ID in person and an ID number on every mail ballot". A verified vote on the
-- VOTES Act locates nobody on that ladder. So 14 rows whose votes ARE verified -- 12 Yeas and
-- Tarr's and O'Connor's Nays -- are blanked with the finding written into the blank. That is the
-- Carson call of migration 1896, and a fifth member of the ladder-scope register.
--
-- ══ ⚠ WHAT THREE EARLIER PASSES WOULD HAVE BLANKED WRONGLY ═══════════════════════════════════
-- Each pass over this cohort rescued rows the previous one would have retired. Recorded because
-- the pattern, not the instances, is the lesson -- it is the same shape as migration 1898's four
-- Maryland passes.
--   1. A PROFILE'S COURT LIST COVERS ONE CHAMBER ONLY. 18 of these 96 members have TWO profile
--      pages because they moved between the branches. Reading only the chamber they sit in now
--      declared Fernandes, Driscoll and Howard "not in the legislature" for votes they had cast
--      as Representatives. Tenure is the UNION of both profiles, and the vote must be looked for
--      in the chamber they were in FOR THAT COURT.
--   2. "RIGHT NUMBER, WRONG COURT" STRUCK AGAIN. H.1795 of the 193rd IS "An Act establishing a
--      jail and prison construction moratorium"; H.1304 of the 193rd IS "An Act enabling local
--      options for tenant protections". Only the 194th versions of those numbers are unrelated.
--      Checking the cited court alone would have blanked Tyler and Lewis, who are on them.
--   3. AN ACT IS REFILED EVERY GENERAL COURT. The Safe Communities Act has been filed in all of
--      190/H3269, 190/S1305, 191/H3573, 191/S1401, 192/H2418, 192/S1579, 193/H2288, 193/S1510,
--      194/H2580, 194/S1681. Michlewitz is on the 190th's filing and on none of the later ones;
--      a 193rd+194th-only check would have blanked a true claim. 🔴 The search parse that hid
--      this: malegislature renders TWO links per result, the bill number then the title, and a
--      parser that required the link text to start with "An Act" silently returned a fraction of
--      the set -- which reads exactly like "this act was only ever filed once".
--   4. "OPPOSED" IS NOT ALWAYS A NO. Daniel J. Hunt was flagged as having voted the opposite of
--      his row because its prose says he "has OPPOSED RESTRICTIONS on abortion access" -- the
--      opposite sense, the same trap as DiDomenico's "default-to-openness" in migration 1899.
--
-- ══ ⚠ ONE MEMBER VOTED BOTH WAYS, AND THE ROW IS NOT WRONG ═══════════════════════════════════
-- Jonathan D. Zlotnik voted NAY on enactment of the police reform act (#285, 91-67) and YEA on
-- re-enactment after the Governor's amendments (#326, 107-51). His row says he "voted for" it,
-- which is true of the vote that made it law. The later vote is taken as the settled position and
-- THE SPLIT IS WRITTEN INTO THE ROW rather than resolved silently.
--
-- ══ 🔴 71 KEYS IN THIS COHORT CANNOT BE WRITTEN AT ALL ════════════════════════════════════════
-- All 71 sit on `immigration`, which has NO Season 2 pin, so there is no open-season row to write
-- and no revision to write it against. They are: all 54 citations to the three wrong Work and
-- Family Mobility Act numbers (192/S2684, 192/S2745, 192/H3256 -- and ⚠ the CORRECT bill for
-- that act was never identified, so even unblocked they would need research first), and 17 Safe
-- Communities citations. Running total of defective chairs unfixable for this reason:
-- migs 1896 (3) + 1897 (3) + 1898 (20) + 1899 (4) + 1900 (71) = 101.
--
-- ══ NOT FIXED, RECORDED ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
--   * 145 citations across 103 bills in the sweep's long tail are still unadjudicated.
--   * 232 MA keys cite ONLY a profile page and need the per-court Sponsored/Cosponsored lists,
--     the way migration 1898 read Maryland's. Untouched here.
--   * ~67 MA rows reason from a NON-SPONSORSHIP or from absence in a third-party tracker; 20 name
--     Act on Mass as evidence without citing it.
--   * 60 of the sweep's low-coverage citations are the METRIC's false alarms, not defects: an
--     acronym whose title spells it out shares no tokens with it ("VOTES Act" vs "fostering Voter
--     Opportunities, Trust, Equity and Security"). Always adjudicate the bill, never the score.
--
-- No migration runner exists; this file records SQL applied by hand via mcp__supabase-local.

