  -- Adrian C. Madaro / jail-capacity  (Season 1 chair 1 -> 1, COSPONSOR_CONFIRMED)
    ('290ee018-6e9e-4a8c-9561-385cf5ef3d73'::uuid, 'c267e137-0ff9-4e7d-9d13-e3cea1756cd0'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, 'f63a4e70-055e-4115-a5e0-3deeb5748816'::uuid, 'Citation repaired 2026-10-07 (migration 1900); THE CHAIR IS UNCHANGED. The claim was right and the citation was wrong. 🔴 THE CITED BILL IS NOT THE ACT. The row cites 194/H1795, which is "An Act relative to prejudgment interest rates". 🔑 THE SPONSORSHIP IS VERIFIED ON THE BILL''S OWN COSPONSORS PAGE: Adrian C. Madaro is a cosponsor of the jail and prison construction moratorium (193/H1795). The claim holds; only the citation was false. ⚠ The search was run across EVERY General Court in which the act has been filed, not only the court the row names: 192/H1905, 192/S2030, 193/H1795, 193/S1979, 193/S2821, 194/H3422, 194/S2114, 194/S2944. A member who signed an earlier filing is a cosponsor of the act whatever the row cites. The sources now point at the cosponsor list that carries the member''s name.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/193/H1795/Cosponsor', 'https://malegislature.gov/Legislators/Profile/ACM1']::text[]),
  -- Adrian C. Madaro / local-immigration  (Season 1 chair 1 -> 1, COSPONSOR_CONFIRMED)
    ('290ee018-6e9e-4a8c-9561-385cf5ef3d73'::uuid, 'b9ccee94-ad96-4f10-b655-889d8e5abe92'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, 'd497a221-1616-4ebf-8405-f9c851083e2c'::uuid, 'Citation repaired 2026-10-07 (migration 1900); THE CHAIR IS UNCHANGED. The claim was right and the citation was wrong. 🔴 THE CITED BILL IS NOT THE ACT. The row cites 194/H3369, which is "An Act relative to school transportation contracts". 🔑 THE SPONSORSHIP IS VERIFIED ON THE BILL''S OWN COSPONSORS PAGE: Adrian C. Madaro is a cosponsor of the Safe Communities Act (190/H3269, 191/H3573, 192/H2418, 193/H2288, 194/H2580). The claim holds; only the citation was false. ⚠ The search was run across EVERY General Court in which the act has been filed, not only the court the row names: 190/H3269, 190/S1305, 191/H3573, 191/S1401, 192/H2418, 192/S1579, 193/H2288, 193/S1510, 194/H2580, 194/S1681. A member who signed an earlier filing is a cosponsor of the act whatever the row cites. The sources now point at the cosponsor list that carries the member''s name.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/190/H3269/Cosponsor', 'https://malegislature.gov/Bills/191/H3573/Cosponsor', 'https://malegislature.gov/Bills/192/H2418/Cosponsor', 'https://malegislature.gov/Bills/193/H2288/Cosponsor', 'https://malegislature.gov/Bills/194/H2580/Cosponsor', 'https://malegislature.gov/Legislators/Profile/ACM1']::text[]),
  -- Brandy Fluker-Reid / abortion  (Season 1 chair 1 -> 0, NOT_SERVING)
    ('5c497509-7a9d-492b-ac35-ff37f8fcfb82'::uuid, 'af2fdfd6-02c4-49df-b09c-cf8536f4773f'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, 'dab46e5c-628a-4360-ad1d-3aaba61768f0'::uuid, 'Blanked 2026-10-07. 🔴🔴 THE MEMBER WAS NOT IN THE LEGISLATURE WHEN THE CLAIMED VOTE HAPPENED. Brandy Fluker-Reid was not in the legislature when abortion-access legislation was voted on: not a member of the 191 General Court, first served in the 194. The claim is impossible, not merely unsupported. The sponsorship limb was also checked: Brandy Fluker-Reid is on none of 193/H1599, 193/S1114, 194/H1815, 194/S1244, 194/S1579. 🔴 THE CITED BILL IS NOT THE ACT. The row cites 194/H1742, which is "An Act to clarify the charitable purposes of certain organizations". The vote limb of these rows concerns the ROE Act, whose own bill -- H.3320 of the 191st -- NEVER REACHED A FLOOR VOTE ("No further action taken", 1/5/2021). The ROE Act became law as H.5179, "An Act providing for access to reproductive health services", section 40 of the FY21 budget, over the Governor''s veto: Chapter 263 of the Acts of 2020. 🔑 CHECK TENURE BEFORE READING A VOTE OR A NON-VOTE -- the rule from the Texas SB 17 pass, which cost two wrong blanks there. ⚠ AND CHECK IT IN BOTH BRANCHES: a profile page lists only the courts served in THAT chamber, so eighteen members of this cohort have a second profile for their earlier House career. Three rows were rescued from this very verdict that way, and this one was tested against both. The sources examined are kept below so the next reader does not re-derive this. This is a blank, not a deletion -- the chair may well be re-researchable, and that is an invitation.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/194/H1742', 'https://malegislature.gov/Legislators/Profile/BFR1']::text[]),
  -- Brendan P. Crighton / voting-rights  (Season 1 chair 1 -> 0, NOT_DISTINCTIVE)
    ('99457307-afa4-4045-aebf-06ee8b39d28f'::uuid, 'd1792200-1d3b-4955-a0b7-0e6980d7a7b2'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, '2a18c152-67a0-4380-a381-cb8795110a7c'::uuid, 'Blanked 2026-10-07. 🔴 THE CITED BILL IS NOT THE ACT. The row cites 192/S2294, which is "An Act relative to tolls on the roadways of the Commonwealth". The VOTES Act was filed as S.2545, reprinted as S.2554 and ENACTED AS S.2924, Chapter 92 of the Acts of 2022. ⚠ AND THE REAL INSTRUMENT CANNOT SEAT THIS CHAIR EITHER. Brendan P. Crighton did vote for the VOTES Act -- Senate roll call #188 (conference report, 06/09/2022), 37-3 -- but 92% of the chamber voted the same way; a near-unanimous vote cannot establish a distinctive position. (the parse was checked against the roll call''s own declared totals before any name was read off it). 🔑 A near-unanimous vote cannot establish a DISTINCTIVE position -- the rule that blanked Newhouse on 385-41 in migration 1894 and Dingell on 417-10 in 1896. A Yea here distinguishes this member from almost nobody. ⚠ THE CONVERSE DOES NOT HOLD, and rows in this same cohort were REPAIRED rather than blanked for exactly that reason: dissent from such a majority IS distinctive. ⚠ Note the asymmetry between the branches on this act -- the same vote was DIVIDED in the other chamber and rows there were kept. The evidence genuinely differs; the verdict follows the evidence, not the act. The sources examined are kept below so the next reader does not re-derive this. This is a blank, not a deletion -- the chair may well be re-researchable, and that is an invitation.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/192/S2294', 'https://malegislature.gov/Bills/192/S2924', 'https://malegislature.gov/RollCall/192/SenateRollCall188.pdf']::text[]),
  -- Brian W. Murray / abortion  (Season 1 chair 1 -> 1, VOTE_CONFIRMED)
    ('4702bc3c-0820-42f4-a0ae-5bc244c44159'::uuid, 'af2fdfd6-02c4-49df-b09c-cf8536f4773f'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, 'dab46e5c-628a-4360-ad1d-3aaba61768f0'::uuid, 'Citation repaired 2026-10-07 (migration 1900); THE CHAIR IS UNCHANGED. The claim was right and the citation was wrong. 🔴 THE CITED BILL IS NOT THE ACT. The row cites 191/H4998, which is "An Act directing the State Retirement Board to grant creditable service to Cheryl A. Bednarik". The ROE Act''s own bill, H.3320 of the 191st, NEVER REACHED A FLOOR VOTE -- "No further action taken", 1/5/2021. The ROE Act became law as H.5179, "An Act providing for access to reproductive health services", enacted as section 40 of the FY21 budget and passed over the Governor''s veto: Chapter 263 of the Acts of 2020. 🔑 THE VOTE IS VERIFIED AGAINST THE PRIMARY RECORD: Brian W. Murray voted for the ROE Act -- House roll call #374 (veto override, 12/28/2020), 107-46. The vote was divided (70% yea), so it is distinctive; only the citation was false. Also a cosponsor of 191/H3320. (the parse was checked against the roll call''s own declared totals before any name was read off it). 🔑 A near-unanimous vote cannot establish a distinctive position -- the rule that blanked Newhouse on 385-41 in migration 1894 and Dingell on 417-10 in 1896 -- so the margin was checked before the chair was kept, not after. The sources now point at the enacted vehicle and the roll call itself.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/191/H5179', 'https://malegislature.gov/Legislators/Profile/BWM1', 'https://malegislature.gov/RollCall/191/HouseRollCall317.pdf', 'https://malegislature.gov/RollCall/191/HouseRollCall374.pdf']::text[]),
  -- Brian W. Murray / public-safety-approach  (Season 1 chair 2 -> 2, VOTE_CONFIRMED)
    ('4702bc3c-0820-42f4-a0ae-5bc244c44159'::uuid, 'e9ebefcd-c496-45e8-b816-a79f8442ba85'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, 'b9c1c07f-f80e-493a-9bb0-015e46c9bc71'::uuid, 'Citation repaired 2026-10-07 (migration 1900); THE CHAIR IS UNCHANGED. The claim was right and the citation was wrong. 🔴 THE CITED BILL IS NOT THE ACT. The row cites 191/H4011, which is "An Act amending the charter of the town of Nantucket". The police reform act was filed as S.2800, reprinted as S.2820 and ENACTED AS S.2963, Chapter 253 of the Acts of 2020. 🔑 THE VOTE IS VERIFIED AGAINST THE PRIMARY RECORD: Brian W. Murray voted for the 2020 police reform act -- House roll call #326 (re-enactment, 12/23/2020), 107-51. The vote was divided (68% yea), so it is distinctive; only the citation was false. (the parse was checked against the roll call''s own declared totals before any name was read off it). 🔑 A near-unanimous vote cannot establish a distinctive position -- the rule that blanked Newhouse on 385-41 in migration 1894 and Dingell on 417-10 in 1896 -- so the margin was checked before the chair was kept, not after. The sources now point at the enacted vehicle and the roll call itself.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/191/S2963', 'https://malegislature.gov/Legislators/Profile/BWM1', 'https://malegislature.gov/RollCall/191/HouseRollCall285.pdf', 'https://malegislature.gov/RollCall/191/HouseRollCall326.pdf']::text[]),
  -- Brian W. Murray / voting-rights  (Season 1 chair 1 -> 0, LADDER_SCOPE)
    ('4702bc3c-0820-42f4-a0ae-5bc244c44159'::uuid, 'd1792200-1d3b-4955-a0b7-0e6980d7a7b2'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, '2a18c152-67a0-4380-a381-cb8795110a7c'::uuid, 'Blanked 2026-10-07, and ⚠ THE UNDERLYING FINDING IS TRUE -- it is recorded here so it is not lost. Brian W. Murray voted for the VOTES Act -- House roll call #200 (enactment, 06/16/2022), 126-29. The vote was divided (81% yea), so it is distinctive; only the citation was false. The Season 2 rung at this value asks a different question. Season 1: "automatically register all eligible citizens to vote and allow online voting" Season 2: "Require no identification to vote, verifying voters by signature or existing records." The finding itself stands and is preserved here, but it cannot seat the Season 2 chair, and a repair may not move a chair. (the parse was checked against the roll call''s own declared totals before any name was read off it). 🔴 THE CITED BILL IS NOT THE ACT. The row cites 192/H5001, which is "An Act increasing diagnostic testing preparedness". The VOTES Act was filed as S.2545, reprinted as S.2554 and ENACTED AS S.2924, Chapter 92 of the Acts of 2022. 🔴🔴 BUT THE SEASON 2 LADDER ASKS A DIFFERENT QUESTION. The Season 2 rung at this value asks a different question. Season 1: "automatically register all eligible citizens to vote and allow online voting" Season 2: "Require no identification to vote, verifying voters by signature or existing records." Season 1 asked about registration, early voting and mail ballots -- the subject of the VOTES Act. Season 2 asks only about VOTER IDENTIFICATION. A verified vote on the VOTES Act locates nobody on a voter-ID scale, so carrying the chair across would seat this member on a position never assessed. That is the Carson call of migration 1896 and the ladder-scope register. A verified fact with nowhere to sit is still a verified fact: it is written here so the next researcher starts from it rather than from nothing. The sources examined are kept below so the next reader does not re-derive this. This is a blank, not a deletion -- the chair may well be re-researchable, and that is an invitation.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/192/H5001', 'https://malegislature.gov/Bills/192/S2924', 'https://malegislature.gov/Legislators/Profile/BWM1/Bills', 'https://malegislature.gov/RollCall/192/HouseRollCall200.pdf']::text[]),
  -- Bruce E. Tarr / voting-rights  (Season 1 chair 4 -> 0, LADDER_SCOPE)
    ('b1bd9a21-a37e-49f4-907b-9fba480a1ca2'::uuid, 'd1792200-1d3b-4955-a0b7-0e6980d7a7b2'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, '2a18c152-67a0-4380-a381-cb8795110a7c'::uuid, 'Blanked 2026-10-07, and ⚠ THE UNDERLYING FINDING IS TRUE -- it is recorded here so it is not lost. Bruce E. Tarr voted NAY on the VOTES Act -- Senate roll call #188 (conference report, 06/09/2022), 37-3 -- and the row says so. Dissent from a 92% majority is distinctive. The Season 2 rung at this value asks a different question. Season 1: "require photo ID for voting and regularly update voter rolls to remove inactive registrations" Season 2: "Require photo ID in person and an ID number on every mail ballot." The finding itself stands and is preserved here, but it cannot seat the Season 2 chair, and a repair may not move a chair. (the parse was checked against the roll call''s own declared totals before any name was read off it). 🔴 THE CITED BILL IS NOT THE ACT. The row cites 192/S2294, which is "An Act relative to tolls on the roadways of the Commonwealth". The VOTES Act was filed as S.2545, reprinted as S.2554 and ENACTED AS S.2924, Chapter 92 of the Acts of 2022. 🔴🔴 BUT THE SEASON 2 LADDER ASKS A DIFFERENT QUESTION. The Season 2 rung at this value asks a different question. Season 1: "require photo ID for voting and regularly update voter rolls to remove inactive registrations" Season 2: "Require photo ID in person and an ID number on every mail ballot." Season 1 asked about registration, early voting and mail ballots -- the subject of the VOTES Act. Season 2 asks only about VOTER IDENTIFICATION. A verified vote on the VOTES Act locates nobody on a voter-ID scale, so carrying the chair across would seat this member on a position never assessed. That is the Carson call of migration 1896 and the ladder-scope register. A verified fact with nowhere to sit is still a verified fact: it is written here so the next researcher starts from it rather than from nothing. The sources examined are kept below so the next reader does not re-derive this. This is a blank, not a deletion -- the chair may well be re-researchable, and that is an invitation.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/192/S2294', 'https://malegislature.gov/Bills/192/S2924', 'https://malegislature.gov/RollCall/192/SenateRollCall188.pdf']::text[]),
