    ('a40f234e-1790-4b52-8670-090b6379eb03'::uuid, 'd1792200-1d3b-4955-a0b7-0e6980d7a7b2'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, '2a18c152-67a0-4380-a381-cb8795110a7c'::uuid, 'Blanked 2026-10-07. 🔴 THE CITED BILL IS NOT THE ACT. The row cites 192/S2294, which is "An Act relative to tolls on the roadways of the Commonwealth". The VOTES Act was filed as S.2545, reprinted as S.2554 and ENACTED AS S.2924, Chapter 92 of the Acts of 2022. ⚠ AND THE REAL INSTRUMENT CANNOT SEAT THIS CHAIR EITHER. Jason M. Lewis did vote for the VOTES Act -- Senate roll call #188 (conference report, 06/09/2022), 37-3 -- but 92% of the chamber voted the same way; a near-unanimous vote cannot establish a distinctive position. (the parse was checked against the roll call''s own declared totals before any name was read off it). 🔑 A near-unanimous vote cannot establish a DISTINCTIVE position -- the rule that blanked Newhouse on 385-41 in migration 1894 and Dingell on 417-10 in 1896. A Yea here distinguishes this member from almost nobody. ⚠ THE CONVERSE DOES NOT HOLD, and rows in this same cohort were REPAIRED rather than blanked for exactly that reason: dissent from such a majority IS distinctive. ⚠ Note the asymmetry between the branches on this act -- the same vote was DIVIDED in the other chamber and rows there were kept. The evidence genuinely differs; the verdict follows the evidence, not the act. The sources examined are kept below so the next reader does not re-derive this. This is a blank, not a deletion -- the chair may well be re-researchable, and that is an invitation.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/192/S2294', 'https://malegislature.gov/Bills/192/S2924', 'https://malegislature.gov/RollCall/192/SenateRollCall188.pdf']::text[]),
