    ('852e4a21-5750-4ac8-ba06-0376c5a31685'::uuid, 'af2fdfd6-02c4-49df-b09c-cf8536f4773f'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, 'dab46e5c-628a-4360-ad1d-3aaba61768f0'::uuid, 'Citation repaired 2026-10-07 (migration 1900); THE CHAIR IS UNCHANGED. The claim was right and the citation was wrong. 🔴 THE CITED BILL IS NOT THE ACT. The row cites 194/H1742, which is "An Act to clarify the charitable purposes of certain organizations". The vote limb of these rows concerns the ROE Act, whose own bill -- H.3320 of the 191st -- NEVER REACHED A FLOOR VOTE ("No further action taken", 1/5/2021). The ROE Act became law as H.5179, "An Act providing for access to reproductive health services", section 40 of the FY21 budget, over the Governor''s veto: Chapter 263 of the Acts of 2020. 🔑 THE VOTE IS VERIFIED AGAINST THE PRIMARY RECORD: Jay Livingstone voted for abortion-access legislation -- House roll call #374 (veto override, 12/28/2020), 107-46. The vote was divided (70% yea), so it is distinctive; only the citation was false. Also a cosponsor of 193/H1599, 194/H1815. (the parse was checked against the roll call''s own declared totals before any name was read off it). 🔑 A near-unanimous vote cannot establish a distinctive position -- the rule that blanked Newhouse on 385-41 in migration 1894 and Dingell on 417-10 in 1896 -- so the margin was checked before the chair was kept, not after. The sources now point at the enacted vehicle and the roll call itself.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/191/H5179', 'https://malegislature.gov/Legislators/Profile/J_L1', 'https://malegislature.gov/RollCall/191/HouseRollCall317.pdf', 'https://malegislature.gov/RollCall/191/HouseRollCall374.pdf']::text[]),
  -- Jeffrey R. Turco / abortion  (Season 1 chair 2 -> 0, NOT_SERVING)
    ('c539c9fa-a531-456f-9125-8d30f1fcedfe'::uuid, 'af2fdfd6-02c4-49df-b09c-cf8536f4773f'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, 'dab46e5c-628a-4360-ad1d-3aaba61768f0'::uuid, 'Blanked 2026-10-07. 🔴🔴 THE MEMBER WAS NOT IN THE LEGISLATURE WHEN THE CLAIMED VOTE HAPPENED. Jeffrey R. Turco was not in the legislature when the ROE Act was voted on: not a member of the 191 General Court, first served in the 192. The claim is impossible, not merely unsupported. 🔴 THE CITED BILL IS NOT THE ACT. The row cites 192/H3320, which is "An Act establishing the GREEN Initiative". The ROE Act''s own bill, H.3320 of the 191st, NEVER REACHED A FLOOR VOTE -- "No further action taken", 1/5/2021. The ROE Act became law as H.5179, "An Act providing for access to reproductive health services", enacted as section 40 of the FY21 budget and passed over the Governor''s veto: Chapter 263 of the Acts of 2020. 🔑 CHECK TENURE BEFORE READING A VOTE OR A NON-VOTE -- the rule from the Texas SB 17 pass, which cost two wrong blanks there. ⚠ AND CHECK IT IN BOTH BRANCHES: a profile page lists only the courts served in THAT chamber, so eighteen members of this cohort have a second profile for their earlier House career. Three rows were rescued from this very verdict that way, and this one was tested against both. The sources examined are kept below so the next reader does not re-derive this. This is a blank, not a deletion -- the chair may well be re-researchable, and that is an invitation.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/192/H3320', 'https://malegislature.gov/Legislators/Profile/JRT1']::text[]),
