    ('799c1cea-4020-4c50-b7d1-1e9aac867529'::uuid, 'e9ebefcd-c496-45e8-b816-a79f8442ba85'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, 'b9c1c07f-f80e-493a-9bb0-015e46c9bc71'::uuid, 'Citation repaired 2026-10-07 (migration 1900); THE CHAIR IS UNCHANGED. The claim was right and the citation was wrong. 🔴 THE CITED BILL IS NOT THE ACT. The row cites 191/H4011, which is "An Act amending the charter of the town of Nantucket". The police reform act was filed as S.2800, reprinted as S.2820 and ENACTED AS S.2963, Chapter 253 of the Acts of 2020. 🔑 THE VOTE IS VERIFIED AGAINST THE PRIMARY RECORD: Paul K. Frost voted NAY on the 2020 police reform act -- House roll call #326 (re-enactment, 12/23/2020), 107-51 -- and the row says so. Dissent from a 68% majority is distinctive. (the parse was checked against the roll call''s own declared totals before any name was read off it). 🔑 THE CONVERSE OF THE LEHMAN RULE: dissent from a lopsided majority IS distinctive, and the dissenters are named in the roll call. The sources now point at the enacted vehicle and the roll call itself.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/191/S2963', 'https://malegislature.gov/Legislators/Profile/PKF1', 'https://malegislature.gov/RollCall/191/HouseRollCall285.pdf', 'https://malegislature.gov/RollCall/191/HouseRollCall326.pdf']::text[]),
  -- Paul R. Feeney / voting-rights  (Season 1 chair 2 -> 0, NOT_DISTINCTIVE)
    ('c435ab14-5d64-46e4-a59f-bba18ed483c9'::uuid, 'd1792200-1d3b-4955-a0b7-0e6980d7a7b2'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, '2a18c152-67a0-4380-a381-cb8795110a7c'::uuid, 'Blanked 2026-10-07. 🔴 THE CITED BILL IS NOT THE ACT. The row cites 192/S2294, which is "An Act relative to tolls on the roadways of the Commonwealth". The VOTES Act was filed as S.2545, reprinted as S.2554 and ENACTED AS S.2924, Chapter 92 of the Acts of 2022. ⚠ AND THE REAL INSTRUMENT CANNOT SEAT THIS CHAIR EITHER. Paul R. Feeney did vote for the VOTES Act -- Senate roll call #188 (conference report, 06/09/2022), 37-3 -- but 92% of the chamber voted the same way; a near-unanimous vote cannot establish a distinctive position. (the parse was checked against the roll call''s own declared totals before any name was read off it). 🔑 A near-unanimous vote cannot establish a DISTINCTIVE position -- the rule that blanked Newhouse on 385-41 in migration 1894 and Dingell on 417-10 in 1896. A Yea here distinguishes this member from almost nobody. ⚠ THE CONVERSE DOES NOT HOLD, and rows in this same cohort were REPAIRED rather than blanked for exactly that reason: dissent from such a majority IS distinctive. ⚠ Note the asymmetry between the branches on this act -- the same vote was DIVIDED in the other chamber and rows there were kept. The evidence genuinely differs; the verdict follows the evidence, not the act. The sources examined are kept below so the next reader does not re-derive this. This is a blank, not a deletion -- the chair may well be re-researchable, and that is an invitation.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/192/S2294', 'https://malegislature.gov/Bills/192/S2924', 'https://malegislature.gov/RollCall/192/SenateRollCall188.pdf']::text[]),
  -- Rob Consalvo / abortion  (Season 1 chair 2 -> 0, NOT_SERVING)
    ('3f5dd4b3-c0b6-470b-861d-41a71637797c'::uuid, 'af2fdfd6-02c4-49df-b09c-cf8536f4773f'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, 'dab46e5c-628a-4360-ad1d-3aaba61768f0'::uuid, 'Blanked 2026-10-07. 🔴🔴 THE MEMBER WAS NOT IN THE LEGISLATURE WHEN THE CLAIMED VOTE HAPPENED. Rob Consalvo was not in the legislature when the ROE Act was voted on: not a member of the 191 General Court, first served in the 192. The claim is impossible, not merely unsupported. The sponsorship limb was also checked: Rob Consalvo is on none of 191/H3320. 🔴 THE CITED BILL IS NOT THE ACT. The row cites 192/H3320, which is "An Act establishing the GREEN Initiative". The ROE Act''s own bill, H.3320 of the 191st, NEVER REACHED A FLOOR VOTE -- "No further action taken", 1/5/2021. The ROE Act became law as H.5179, "An Act providing for access to reproductive health services", enacted as section 40 of the FY21 budget and passed over the Governor''s veto: Chapter 263 of the Acts of 2020. 🔑 CHECK TENURE BEFORE READING A VOTE OR A NON-VOTE -- the rule from the Texas SB 17 pass, which cost two wrong blanks there. ⚠ AND CHECK IT IN BOTH BRANCHES: a profile page lists only the courts served in THAT chamber, so eighteen members of this cohort have a second profile for their earlier House career. Three rows were rescued from this very verdict that way, and this one was tested against both. The sources examined are kept below so the next reader does not re-derive this. This is a blank, not a deletion -- the chair may well be re-researchable, and that is an invitation.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/192/H3320', 'https://malegislature.gov/Legislators/Profile/R_C1']::text[]),
  -- Russell E. Holmes / abortion  (Season 1 chair 1 -> 0, INVERTED_VOTE)
    ('e96b35eb-dad1-4a29-b416-d9b10838840f'::uuid, 'af2fdfd6-02c4-49df-b09c-cf8536f4773f'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, 'dab46e5c-628a-4360-ad1d-3aaba61768f0'::uuid, 'Blanked 2026-10-07. 🔴🔴 THE ROW STATES THE OPPOSITE OF THE RECORD. The row says Russell E. Holmes supported abortion-access legislation. Holmes voted NAY -- House roll call #374 (veto override, 12/28/2020), 107-46. The chair states the opposite of the record. (the parse was checked against the roll call''s own declared totals before any name was read off it; there is exactly one member of this surname on the roll call, so no disambiguation was required). 🔴 THE CITED BILL IS NOT THE ACT. The row cites 194/H1742, which is "An Act to clarify the charitable purposes of certain organizations". The vote limb of these rows concerns the ROE Act, whose own bill -- H.3320 of the 191st -- NEVER REACHED A FLOOR VOTE ("No further action taken", 1/5/2021). The ROE Act became law as H.5179, "An Act providing for access to reproductive health services", section 40 of the FY21 budget, over the Governor''s veto: Chapter 263 of the Acts of 2020. 🔑 Retiring this row on the false citation alone would have been right for the wrong reason, and trusting the citation would have left an INVERTED chair standing. Only the primary record separates the two. The vote is verified and genuinely distinctive, but the chair was seated on the belief that the member voted the other way, so there is no chair to keep: re-seating on the true vote is new research, not a repair. The sources examined are kept below so the next reader does not re-derive this. This is a blank, not a deletion -- the chair may well be re-researchable, and that is an invitation.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/191/H5179', 'https://malegislature.gov/Bills/194/H1742', 'https://malegislature.gov/Legislators/Profile/REH1', 'https://malegislature.gov/RollCall/191/HouseRollCall317.pdf', 'https://malegislature.gov/RollCall/191/HouseRollCall374.pdf']::text[]),
  -- Russell E. Holmes / jail-capacity  (Season 1 chair 1 -> 1, COSPONSOR_CONFIRMED)
    ('e96b35eb-dad1-4a29-b416-d9b10838840f'::uuid, 'c267e137-0ff9-4e7d-9d13-e3cea1756cd0'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, 'f63a4e70-055e-4115-a5e0-3deeb5748816'::uuid, 'Citation repaired 2026-10-07 (migration 1900); THE CHAIR IS UNCHANGED. The claim was right and the citation was wrong. 🔴 THE CITED BILL IS NOT THE ACT. The row cites 194/H1795, which is "An Act relative to prejudgment interest rates". 🔑 THE SPONSORSHIP IS VERIFIED ON THE BILL''S OWN COSPONSORS PAGE: Russell E. Holmes is a cosponsor of the jail and prison construction moratorium (193/S1979, 194/S2114). The claim holds; only the citation was false. ⚠ The search was run across EVERY General Court in which the act has been filed, not only the court the row names: 192/H1905, 192/S2030, 193/H1795, 193/S1979, 193/S2821, 194/H3422, 194/S2114, 194/S2944. A member who signed an earlier filing is a cosponsor of the act whatever the row cites. The sources now point at the cosponsor list that carries the member''s name.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/193/S1979/Cosponsor', 'https://malegislature.gov/Bills/194/S2114/Cosponsor', 'https://malegislature.gov/Legislators/Profile/REH1']::text[]),
