-- Adam Gómez / housing  (Season 2 row exists at 1; 1 -> 0, NOT_DISTINCTIVE)
UPDATE inform.politician_context SET reasoning = 'Blanked 2026-10-07. 🔴 THE CITED BILL IS NOT THE ACT. The row cites 193/SD3030, which is "An Act authorizing the town of Rochester to continue the employment of Scott Weigel". The Affordable Homes Act is H.4977, Chapter 150 of the Acts of 2024 -- the one act in this cohort whose familiar number IS the enacted vehicle. ⚠ AND THE REAL INSTRUMENT CANNOT SEAT THIS CHAIR EITHER. Adam Gómez did vote for the Affordable Homes Act -- Senate roll call #249 (enactment, 08/01/2024), 37-2 -- but 95% of the chamber voted the same way; a near-unanimous vote cannot establish a distinctive position. (the parse was checked against the roll call''s own declared totals before any name was read off it). 🔑 A near-unanimous vote cannot establish a DISTINCTIVE position -- the rule that blanked Newhouse on 385-41 in migration 1894 and Dingell on 417-10 in 1896. A Yea here distinguishes this member from almost nobody. ⚠ THE CONVERSE DOES NOT HOLD, and rows in this same cohort were REPAIRED rather than blanked for exactly that reason: dissent from such a majority IS distinctive. ⚠ Note the asymmetry between the branches on this act -- the same vote was DIVIDED in the other chamber and rows there were kept. The evidence genuinely differs; the verdict follows the evidence, not the act. The sources examined are kept below so the next reader does not re-derive this. This is a blank, not a deletion -- the chair may well be re-researchable, and that is an invitation.', sources = ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/193/H4977', 'https://malegislature.gov/Bills/193/SD3030', 'https://malegislature.gov/Legislators/Profile/A_G0', 'https://malegislature.gov/RollCall/193/SenateRollCall249.pdf']::text[], updated_at = now()
